      "text": "To find the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\), we can first express \\(12!\\) in terms of \\(11!\\):\n\n\\[12! = 12 \\times 11!\\]\n\nNow, we can rewrite the expression \\(11! + 12!\\):\n\n\\[11! + 12! = 11! + 12 \\times 11!\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[11! + 12 \\times 11! = 11!(1 + 12)\\]\n\\[= 11! \\times 13\\]\n\nNow we need to find the prime factors of the expression \\(11! \\times 13\\). Since \\(11!\\) is the factorial of 11, it is the product of all integers from 1 to 11, including 13 (as 13 is a prime number). This means that the largest prime divisor here is the same as the largest prime factor of 11!, which is 13.\n\nTherefore, the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\) is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).",
