      "text": "To find the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\), we start by expressing \\(12!\\) in terms of \\(11!\\):\n\\[\n12! = 12 \\times 11!\n\\]\nThus, the expression \\(11! + 12!\\) can be written as:\n\\[\n11! + 12! = 11! (1 + 12) = 11! \\times 13\n\\]\nHere, \\(11!\\) and 13 are the factors of the expression. We know that \\(11!\\) is the product of all integers from 1 to 11, so it is divisible by every integer up to 11. Among these factors, 13 is the largest prime number.\n\nTo confirm that 13 is a prime divisor, we note that all integers greater than 13 can be divided by prime numbers less than or equal to 13. Since \\(11!\\) is divisible by 13, 11!, 12!, and 13! are clearly larger than \\(11! + 12!\\), and therefore 13 is the largest prime factor of \\(11! + 12!\\).\n\nThus, the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\) is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).",
